l7a1045: the ADC hands over one word per conversion, not a block at a time

On the SAMPLE screen the input level bars drop to their bottom segment and
come back, over and over, while a steady tone is being played in. The audio
is not the problem - stereo separation and the absence of sidebands are both
measured good, and 0056 settled the buzz. This is the meter.

BOTH BARS, ALWAYS TOGETHER. That is the clue that names the fault. A level
that really varied would move the two channels independently; measured over
2801 frames the two agreed on "at the bottom" in 99.9% of them. Something was
zeroing them from one place.

That place is in the firmware, and it is not a level at all. The meter is a
scan of the 0x7F000 capture ring, and it scans from where it stopped last time
up to where DMA channel 2 has got to now:

    0x49738  mov si,[0x8c12]      ; where the last scan stopped
    0x4973e  call 0x91d4          ; where channel 2 has got to now
    0x49741  and al,0xfe          ; forced to an even word index
    0x49743  mov [0x8c12],ax
    0x49746  xor di,di            ; running maximum starts at zero
    0x4974b  cmp byte [0xd7cd],2  ; stereo?
    0x49752  cmp ax,si            ;   STEREO: has the pointer moved?
    0x49756  jmp 0x97d9           ;     no -> report zero, for BOTH channels
    0x497a6  cmp ax,si            ;   MONO: has the pointer moved?
    0x497a8  jz 0x97d6            ;     no -> report zero

and 0x491d4 is where the pointer comes from - channel 2's CURRENT ADDRESS
register, read under CLI so no interrupt can get between the select and the
read:

    mov ax,2 / mov dx,0xc031 / out dx,al   ; select channel 2
    mov dx,0xc034 / in ax,dx               ; CURRENT ADDRESS
    lea ax,[si+0x1000] / shr ax,1          ; -> word index 0..0x3ff

A pointer that has not moved is a scan window of zero length, and a zero-length
window reports silence on both channels at once out of the same xor di,di. The
scanned maximum is folded into the bar by MAXIMUM, so a skipped scan does not
push the bar down by itself; it fails to hold it up, and the periodic decay at
0x496f5 takes it to the floor.

SO THE METER IS ONLY AS STEADY AS THE ADDRESS REGISTER, AND OURS WAS NOT.
0051 drives channel 2 from a level DRQ that is asserted whenever the capture
ring is not empty. record_capture() fills that ring a whole sound-stream block
at a time - thirty frames, sixty stereo words - so the DMAC takes all sixty
back to back at bus speed and is then asked for nothing for the rest of the
block period. The words are right, the rate is right, and the address register
leaps and then stands still.

On the real machine it does neither. The converter produces one word per
enabled channel per sample period and each word raises DRQ by itself, so the
address creeps forward every 11.3us and never stops while the input is armed.

MEASURED, at the source, by counting what the guest's own read returns.
Instrumented on upd71071_device::read for offset 4 with channel 2 selected -
the exact access 0x491d4 makes - on the SAMPLE screen with a steady 440Hz-left
/ 660Hz-right probe:

    meter polls of the address register      10,700 per second
    polls where the word index had not moved 80%
    of those, with no channel-2 transfer     99.6%
    words channel 2 moved                    88,200 per second

Eight words of movement per poll is what the real converter delivers. Nothing
at all, four polls in five, is what the burst delivered.

THE FIX IS THE CONVERSION CLOCK, NOT THE METER. A timer at sample_rate x
channels makes one word available per conversion; m_adc_due is the converted
word the DMAC has not collected yet, capped at one frame because the converter
holds a frame and not a queue; and DRQ is asserted only when a converted word
is both due and present in the ring. Nothing about the meter, the bars or the
decay is touched, and nothing is smoothed or held.

The property the level DRQ was chosen for survives: DRQ is still never raised
over an empty ring, so the DMAC is never handed a fabricated sample and still
takes exactly as many words as the stream produced. starved stays 0.

A/B ON ONE BINARY, both arms the same build with the same graph reset before
each, run in both orders, on a quiet machine (load 2.7). 2801 exported LCD
frames per arm at 80/s, no missed sequence numbers, counting frames where both
bars are at their bottom segment:

                                    before      after
    both bars at the bottom         61.7%       0.0%
    address polls that saw no move  81.7%       0.0%
    smallest bar seen (of 887 px)   52          472
    median bar                      52          886
    words channel 2 moved           88,200/s    88,200/s
    starved                         0           0

The 61.7% is the figure a run under load put at 63.5%, so the earlier number
was not a load artefact.

Channel 3's phase against channel 2 tightens as a side effect, because the
thing it was chasing stopped jumping: the lag 0056 measures went from [59,114]
to [59,60], one block, flat.

The rig is unchanged at all four stages; the switch that bypassed the clock
existed only for the A/B above and is not in this diff.
