+// THE ADC HAS A CONVERSION CLOCK, AND THE RING POINTER MOVES AT ITS RATE.
+//
+// On the real machine the converter produces one word per enabled channel per
+// sample period and each word raises DRQ on DMA channel 2 by itself, so the
+// channel's CURRENT ADDRESS register creeps forward every 11.3us and never
+// stands still while the input is armed.
+//
+// That register is not private. The SAMPLE screen's level meter is a scan of
+// the 0x7F000 ring bounded by it: the module reads it at 0x491d4
+//
+//     mov ax,2 / mov dx,0xc031 / out dx,al   ; select channel 2
+//     mov dx,0xc034 / in ax,dx               ; CURRENT ADDRESS
+//     lea ax,[si+0x1000] / shr ax,1          ; -> word index 0..0x3ff
+//
+// and then, at 0x49752 (stereo) and 0x497a6 (mono), compares it against the
+// index it scanned to last time and SKIPS THE SCAN ENTIRELY if they are
+// equal. A skipped scan reports zero for both channels at once, which is why
+// the left and right bars always emptied together and never separately.
+//
+// Draining the ring on nothing but "not empty" froze that pointer for the
+// rest of every block period. Measured on the SAMPLE screen with a steady
+// input, the meter polls the register 10,700 times a second and found it
+// unchanged on 80% of those polls - and in 99.6% of those the channel had not
+// transferred at all since the previous poll. Eight words of movement per
+// poll is what the real converter delivers; nothing at all four times in five
+// is what the burst delivered.
+//
+// So this timer is the converter, not a throttle. One tick makes one word
+// available; m_adc_due is the word it has converted and the DMAC has not
+// collected yet, and it is capped at one frame because the converter holds a
+// frame, not a queue - if the DMAC has not taken the last one by the next
+// conversion, the emulated ADC simply runs a frame slow and the ring, which
+// exists to absorb the difference between the host's block delivery and this
+// clock, gives it back.
+void l7a1045_sound_device::arm_adc_timer()
+{
+	if (!m_rec_on)
+	{
+		m_adc_timer->adjust(attotime::never);
+		m_adc_due = 0;
+		return;
+	}
+	const u32 channels = (m_rec_left && m_rec_right) ? 2 : 1;
+	const auto period = attotime::from_hz(m_sample_rate * channels);
+	m_adc_timer->adjust(period, 0, period);
+}
+
+TIMER_CALLBACK_MEMBER(l7a1045_sound_device::adc_timer_callback)
+{
+	const u32 channels = (m_rec_left && m_rec_right) ? 2 : 1;
+	if (m_adc_due < channels)
+		m_adc_due++;
+	update_adc_drq();
+}
+
